# OWNER-LOGIN DEPLOYMENT — Compose INTERPOLATION values for docker-compose.image.yml and its overrides
# (docker compose --env-file <this file>). The ONLY secrets here are the database values below (owner decision 2026-11-18,
# Twenty's way: like Twenty's compose, the passwords are env values, `openssl rand -hex 32`; no container ever receives
# PG_DATABASE_PASSWORD or PG_DATABASE_APP_PASSWORD by name, only the one-shot db-setup and db-harden services receive
# PG_ADMIN_URL, and db-setup, db-harden, rab-api and rab-worker all receive the same PG_DATABASE_URL, the crewflow_owner
# URL); otherwise only image
# references, host paths, the API bind address/port and resource limits. Copy to e.g. /etc/crewflow/compose.env
# (root:root 0600). Passing --env-file also stops Compose from reading a stray .env next to the compose files.
# Lint it: node packages/rab-docker/scripts/compose-owner-login.cjs --lint-env-file compose /etc/crewflow/compose.env
#
# DATABASE (owner decision 2026-11-18, "change PR #125 option 2 to Twenty's way"). rab-api and rab-worker read
# PG_DATABASE_URL, ALWAYS the crewflow_owner URL (direct host, sslmode=verify-full), from their environment, as Twenty's server
# and worker read the app user's URL; only the one-shot db-setup holds an admin login. No file carries a credential (no
# target file, no password file). Choose ONE of three modes. No psql, no manual step (except mode c).
#
# (a) BUNDLED db (the default; CREWFLOW_BUNDLED_DB unset or 1). REQUIRED in this mode only, each 32+ characters of
#     A-Z a-z 0-9 _ - (generate each: openssl rand -hex 32), and leave PG_DATABASE_URL / PG_ADMIN_URL unset:
#       PG_DATABASE_PASSWORD      the bundled db's superuser password (db-setup's admin login). Read only on the db volume's
#                                 first start; changing it later needs ALTER ROLE postgres in the db container first.
#       PG_DATABASE_APP_PASSWORD  the crewflow_owner password. Compose builds
#                                 postgresql://crewflow_owner:<it>@db:5432/crewflow?sslmode=verify-full for db-setup,
#                                 rab-api and rab-worker; db-setup creates crewflow_owner with it (and sets it again when
#                                 crewflow_owner exists and it does not log in). Keep it: it IS the app login's password.
#     A missing value is refused at start (db, db-setup, rab-api, rab-worker) and by the lint.
PG_DATABASE_PASSWORD=<openssl-rand-hex-32>
PG_DATABASE_APP_PASSWORD=<openssl-rand-hex-32>
#
# YOUR OWN database: CREWFLOW_BUNDLED_DB=0 (like Twenty's Helm db.enabled=false: `db` gets 0 replicas and never starts;
# OpenShip ignores the replica count, so there switch the db service off in OpenShip itself),
# REMOVE PG_DATABASE_PASSWORD above (not needed, and refused by the lint: no dummy value):
# CREWFLOW_BUNDLED_DB=0
# (b) owner decision 2026-11-19, "You set one shared password" (the canonical own-database mode): PG_ADMIN_URL is your
#     database's ADMIN URL, pasted AS-IS from your provider (db-setup ONLY; rab-api and rab-worker never receive it): the
#     login must be able to create roles (CREATEROLE or superuser) and to grant on the database. KEEP PG_DATABASE_APP_PASSWORD
#     above (openssl rand -hex 32: the crewflow_owner password) and copy the database's DIRECT host, port and name from the
#     admin URL into PG_DATABASE_HOST / PG_DATABASE_PORT / PG_DATABASE_NAME: compose builds the crewflow_owner URL
#     (PG_DATABASE_URL) from them for db-setup, rab-api and rab-worker alike. db-setup creates crewflow_owner with that
#     password (pre-hashed; a server that refuses pre-hashed passwords gets crewflow_owner WITHOUT a password and NOLOGIN, and db-setup
#     stops with fixed steps: run the printed SQL (your value in one set_config line, then a DO block), all lines together in
#     one go, once, then redeploy; Crewflow never sends a plain-text password; the typed value can stay in server logs and
#     provider logs), sets it when crewflow_owner exists and it does not
#     log in, and removes CREATEROLE once the schema is complete.
# PG_ADMIN_URL=<your provider's admin connection string, as-is>
# PG_DATABASE_HOST=<db-direct-host>
# PG_DATABASE_PORT=5432
# PG_DATABASE_NAME=<db-name>
#     If PG_ADMIN_URL is a pooled address (a connection pooler: db-setup refuses it by what it does, not by its name), keep it
#     and add the DIRECT address of the same login and database (db-setup and db-harden only):
# PG_ADMIN_DIRECT_URL=postgresql://<admin-login>:<password>@<db-direct-host>:5432/<db-name>?sslmode=verify-full
#     Alternative to PG_DATABASE_APP_PASSWORD + PG_DATABASE_HOST/PORT/NAME (never both): the whole crewflow_owner URL,
#     direct host, sslmode=verify-full:
# PG_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://crewflow_owner:<openssl-rand-hex-32>@<db-direct-host>:5432/<db-name>?sslmode=verify-full
# (c) PG_DATABASE_URL only: the crewflow_owner URL you created yourself (postgres-init/owner-login/own-database-app-login.sql),
#     and NO PG_ADMIN_URL. db-setup does no provisioning: it only checks it, read-only (never SUPERUSER / BYPASSRLS,
#     PostgreSQL 16+), and never changes the database or the password.
# PG_DATABASE_URL must log in as crewflow_owner (any other login is refused: an admin URL goes in PG_ADMIN_URL), and
# PG_ADMIN_URL must not log in as crewflow_owner. PG_DATABASE_ADMIN_URL (the admin URL's former name) is refused.
#
# CREATEROLE hardening (owner decision 2026-11-18, security review of PR #246, M1). Modes a and b: the one-shot db-harden
# service (same image, the same database settings as db-setup, started with the stack, nothing waits for it) waits
# read-only until the API has applied every migration, then removes CREATEROLE from crewflow_owner and exits; mode c: it
# does nothing. OPTIONAL: how long it waits, in whole seconds from 60 to 7200 (unset = 1200). When it times out it exits
# non-zero and changes nothing; the next start of db-setup removes CREATEROLE once the schema is complete.
# CREWFLOW_DB_HARDEN_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=1200
#
# How long db-setup (and db-harden) wait for the database (bundled or yours) to accept connections before they give up
# (exit 70; nothing is changed): whole seconds 0-900, unset = 170 (inside OpenShip's 180 s window for rab-api's dependency
# on db-setup), 0 = do not wait. Neither has depends_on: db (owner instruction 2026-11-18, Twenty's Helm wait-for-db), so
# they wait for the database themselves: the bundled db, or PG_ADMIN_URL's host when it is set, else PG_DATABASE_URL's.
# CREWFLOW_DB_WAIT_SECONDS=170
#
# Image: the approved release's multi-platform INDEX digest (amd64 + arm64). The variable is named like Twenty's TAG but
# accepts ONLY a digest (sha256:<64 hex>); a plain tag is refused. ONE image, ONE variable runs the API (which migrates on
# start, O3), the Worker and the owner CLI one-off commands run from rab-api (O1/O2, X2.2; published under the one tag
# <full commit>; take the digest from the release manifest). Deploy: `docker compose pull && docker compose up -d`.
# Rollback: put the previous digest here, same command.
# Refused here: CREWFLOW_IMAGE_DIGEST (this variable's name until 2026-11-11) and any per-role variable
# (CREWFLOW_API_IMAGE_* / CREWFLOW_WORKER_IMAGE_*): there is no API-only or Worker-only deploy.
CREWFLOW_IMAGE_TAG=sha256:<image-index-digest-64-hex>
# Only to override the release registry (default adhin007/adolphus):
# CREWFLOW_IMAGE_REPOSITORY=<registry/repository>
#
# Migration approval (BC-11, C4): unset = none, the API applies pending migrations on start without a token (like
# Twenty). token = opt-in safety net: an API start that would apply to a non-fresh database needs
# CREWFLOW_MIGRATION_APPROVED=<token> for exactly the pending set (passed to that one start only, never in a file). Set it
# HERE only: the lint refuses it in database.env / runtime.env, where rab-api's committed value would override it.
# CREWFLOW_MIGRATION_APPROVAL_MODE=token
# Redis 7.x, pinned by digest; record the tag it was resolved from.
CREWFLOW_REDIS_IMAGE=redis@sha256:<redis-7-alpine-index-digest-64-hex>
# Only to override the bundled db's committed image (postgres:16, Debian, by digest): another Debian postgres:16, :17 or :18 digest (never alpine: the volume layout is the Debian one).
# CREWFLOW_POSTGRES_IMAGE=postgres@sha256:<postgres-index-digest-64-hex>
#
# Host paths (absolute). The secret directory (root 0700) holds database.env, runtime.env and redis-users.acl.
CREWFLOW_SECRETS_DIR=/etc/crewflow/secrets
# EXTERNAL database only, when its CA is not in the system store: the provider's CA bundle (mounted at
# /run/crewflow/pg-ca.crt; set PG_SSL_CA_CERT_PATH to that path in database.env). The bundled db needs none. Without a file
# to mount, put the PEM text in database.env as PG_SSL_CA_CERT instead (and leave this unset).
# CREWFLOW_PG_CA_FILE=/etc/crewflow/pg-ca.crt
#
# The address and host port the edge proxy connects to. Never 0.0.0.0. Pick a free host port when another service already
# uses 3000 on this host (Oracle VM: OWNER-LOGIN-ORACLE-VM-PLAN.md).
CREWFLOW_API_BIND=127.0.0.1
CREWFLOW_API_HOST_PORT=3000
#
# Resource limits (cpus must not exceed the host's vCPU count; Docker refuses to start the container otherwise).
CREWFLOW_API_MEM_LIMIT=1024m
CREWFLOW_API_CPUS=2
CREWFLOW_WORKER_MEM_LIMIT=1536m
CREWFLOW_WORKER_CPUS=2
CREWFLOW_REDIS_MAXMEMORY=384mb
CREWFLOW_POSTGRES_MEM_LIMIT=1024m
CREWFLOW_POSTGRES_CPUS=2
#
# MUST NOT BE SET here: any other password, URL, token (CREWFLOW_MIGRATION_APPROVED included) or secret, and any
# application variable (NODE_ENV, other PG_*, REDIS_URL, APP_SECRET, CREWFLOW_DB_MODE, ...). PG_DATABASE_URL,
# PG_ADMIN_URL and PG_DATABASE_APP_PASSWORD belong HERE only (the lint refuses them in database.env / runtime.env). COMPOSE_FILE / COMPOSE_PROFILES /
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME: name the files explicitly with -f instead.
